### Change Control
Issue Date | Version | Details | Author
---|---|---|---
2026-09-17 | 1.0 | Initial publication of comprehensive enterprise test strategy for avatar upload, crop, validation, security, storage, CDN synchronization, and release risk controls. | Senior QA Director / Release Risk Manager

## 1. Scope
This test strategy governs Issue AD-488 and defines the quality assurance approach for the authenticated avatar upload and crop capability used for public profile display. The scope is intentionally aligned to the approved business boundaries and technical architecture already established upstream in the pipeline. The feature under test begins with authenticated access gating and ends only when a validated avatar is visible across user-facing presentation surfaces after storage and CDN synchronization. Accordingly, the strategy covers the end-to-end path of UI access control, upload initiation, crop flow participation within the update journey, API Gateway routing, server-side binary transfer and processing, malware inspection, header and magic-number validation, cloud bucket persistence, cache invalidation, and dynamic user interface propagation without full page reload.

From a business perspective, the scope exists to protect public identity consistency while preventing security, storage, and synchronization failures from degrading member trust. The story is not merely a file upload function; it is an identity publication workflow with direct user-visible consequences and a strict latency target. The test program therefore validates not only positive user interaction but also architectural guarantees: that invalid sessions are rejected before use, that unsupported or spoofed files are blocked before persistence, that malware-positive objects never enter long-term storage, and that successful updates propagate to all active avatar surfaces in near-real time. This section also preserves the low-risk guardrails established by acceptance criteria, especially the hard 5MB threshold, the JPEG/WebP whitelist, and the 2.0-second end-to-end SLA under standard 4G conditioning.

In scope are authenticated avatar upload operations, avatar cropping within the story flow, UI gating based on valid session cookies, gateway-level filtering, server-side-only processing, synchronous malware scanning, header-byte and magic-number verification, file size boundary enforcement, specialized cloud bucket storage, immediate CDN invalidation, synchronization across active user views and header bars, dynamic DOM refresh, and gateway rejection handling with 401 Unauthorized behavior. Out-of-scope items remain excluded from positive design and execution to prevent requirement dilution: client-side compression, local image scaling engines, browser canvas processing, unsupported image formats such as PNG/SVG/GIF/raw design files except for rejection validation, concurrent batch uploads, multi-image profile adjustments, and storage infrastructure outside the authorized cloud ecosystem.

## 2. Test Objectives
The primary objective is to verify that the avatar update capability is secure, functionally correct, operationally resilient, and release-ready under the constrained architecture approved for this story. Testing must prove that only authenticated users with fresh, unexpired session tokens can discover and interact with the avatar modification entry points. It must also prove that the dedicated API Gateway remains the enforcement boundary for invalid sessions and over-limit requests, returning appropriate rejection responses while preserving consistent user-facing behavior. Functional correctness extends beyond accepting valid image uploads; it includes confirming crop participation in the update journey, validating exact boundary acceptance at 5.00MB, rejecting 5.01MB and above, restricting acceptance to JPEG and WebP, and detecting renamed or structurally invalid payloads through binary inspection rather than extension-based trust.

A second objective is to validate the integrity of the processing chain and ensure architectural conformance. The system design requires server-side-only execution, synchronous malware inspection prior to persistence, write operations into specialized cloud bucket folders, and immediate public CDN cache invalidation after successful persistence. Testing must therefore establish evidence that no rejected object is written, that the correct storage target is used, that cache purge events are triggered reliably, and that users do not see stale avatars after success. The objective also includes explicit coverage of the identified review gaps from prior pipeline validation: crop correctness must be validated through coordinate mapping and resulting persisted variant checks, and the oversized upload case must explicitly assert the presence of the specified UI error banner when a 5.01MB-or-greater request is dropped at the gateway.

A third objective is non-functional confidence. The full upload, scan, store, invalidate, and distribute chain must complete within 2.0 seconds in a 4G-constrained environment. This demands instrumentation, timed checkpoints, and evidence-based SLA reporting rather than anecdotal observation. Testing must also confirm no full browser reload occurs during successful avatar refresh and that active user views update through dynamic DOM behavior. Collectively, these objectives ensure that quality assessment is tied to user experience, architecture, security controls, and release risk reduction.

## 3. Quality Objectives
Quality for this release is defined as the intersection of identity fidelity, security enforcement, runtime performance, and propagation consistency. The avatar image is a high-visibility profile element, so any defect in validation, persistence, or synchronization has disproportionate reputational impact relative to the apparent simplicity of the UI. Therefore, the quality target is not limited to “upload works”; instead, it requires demonstrable confidence that the correct users can act, the correct files are accepted, malicious or malformed inputs are blocked, and valid outputs become visible without stale cache artifacts. This means quality objectives include strict authentication gating, deterministic file-type verification through magic numbers, exact file-size boundary enforcement, synchronous scan-before-store behavior, and observable cache invalidation across profile render surfaces.

The release also carries a strong engineering-quality objective: adherence to the intended architecture must be verifiable in tests. Because the implementation prohibits client-side compression and processing, any front-end behavior suggesting canvas manipulation, pre-transformation, or local file mutation would represent a design breach even if the UI appeared to function. Likewise, any asynchronous malware processing pattern that allows temporary persistence before verdict would be unacceptable. Tests are therefore designed not only to validate outputs but to verify pathway integrity, including gateway logs, service responses, storage audit traces, and client-side event observations. This objective reduces latent defects that often escape purely UI-oriented regression suites.

Finally, operational quality is measured by release control discipline. The strategy incorporates traceability from acceptance criteria to tests, mandatory evidence retention, structured defect triage, and integration into CI/CD gates so that regression risk is visible before deployment. Human-in-the-Loop approvals remain essential at control gates, particularly where environment readiness, architecture alignment, and release authorization must be explicitly validated. These quality objectives support a release decision based on measurable evidence, not inferred completeness.

#### 3.2.2 Out of Scope
Client-side compression tools, local image scaling engines, and front-end canvas manipulation scripts are outside scope because the approved architecture explicitly forbids browser-side image processing. Unsupported formats such as PNG, SVG, GIF, and raw design files are also outside positive feature scope; they will only be used as negative-validation inputs to confirm rejection behavior and will not be expanded into compatibility commitments.

Concurrent batch uploads, multi-image profile header changes, and deployment/storage patterns outside the authorized cloud ecosystem are excluded because they represent distinct product and infrastructure capabilities not approved for this story. Performance certification beyond the stated 2.0-second requirement under standard 4G conditions is not part of this release objective. Broader resilience activities such as chaos testing, disaster recovery certification, and enterprise network failover testing remain placeholders unless separately authorized.

## 4. Test Approach
The testing approach follows a layered execution model spanning Component/UI, API, Service/Integration, System End-to-End, External Integration, and Non-Functional/Performance levels. Requirement-based coverage mapping will be used so that every acceptance criterion and architectural constraint is tied to executable tests and review evidence. Equivalence partitioning will segment valid and invalid authentication states, supported and unsupported file formats, accepted and rejected file-size ranges, clean versus malware-positive payloads, and successful versus failed propagation paths. Boundary value analysis will focus heavily on the 5MB threshold, ensuring precise validation at below-limit, exact-limit, and above-limit conditions.

Decision table testing will model the most critical outcome combinations across authentication state, file size, file type, malware result, and persistence eligibility. State transition testing will be applied to the avatar flow states of gated-hidden, enabled, uploading, scanning, persisted, propagated, and rejected. Negative testing will intentionally exercise expired sessions, missing tokens, malformed binaries, spoofed extensions, corrupted headers, malware-positive objects, stale CDN artifacts, and oversized files. Explicit additional design attention will be given to crop correctness, including coordinate integrity, persistence of the cropped variant rather than the original, and rendering of the resulting asset across dependent UI surfaces.

Execution will proceed only after Human-in-the-Loop validation is granted for environment and architecture readiness. Once authorized, automation and targeted manual checks will run in coordinated waves, producing linked evidence across gateway logs, browser traces, service responses, storage audit logs, and CDN purge verification.

#### 4.1.1 Test Types
*Describe all the test types in this project and provide the general testing timeline.*

For this release, not every enterprise test type in the base template is applicable as a full workstream; however, all relevant rows are preserved to respect template structure. Core delivery emphasis will be on automation, functional/integration, performance, security, journey, IT E2E, and regression testing. Early-cycle execution will prioritize unit/configuration checks and API/service contract validation in Dev and QA. Mid-cycle efforts will intensify around functional, integration, security, and data-quality assertions in QA. Final pre-release confidence will focus on journey, end-to-end, regression, UAT/Ops alignment, and selective production smoke readiness.

The timeline alignment is as follows: Dev supports component and service verification during feature completion; QA hosts the main automation and exploratory execution wave; UAT confirms business-facing behavior and operational acceptability; Pre-Prod validates deployment-equivalent flow including CDN invalidation and network conditioning; Prod is limited to tightly scoped smoke validation after release approval. Tools embedded in this strategy include Playwright for UI and DOM synchronization checks, PyTest for API/service and binary-validation assertions, JMeter for SLA and throughput timing under 4G network constraints, OWASP ZAP for authenticated security inspection of the upload path, and tcconfig for deterministic Linux-based network shaping to simulate 4G conditions in controlled pipelines.

