### 4.2 Requirement Traceability Matrix
Requirement traceability will be maintained from acceptance criteria, architectural constraints, and identified review gaps directly to manual and automated tests, defects, execution evidence, and release sign-off artifacts. Each requirement will receive a unique trace key derived from Issue AD-488 and mapped in Jira/Xray or an equivalent test management layer. Stories, sub-tasks, and defects will link back to the authoritative trace record so that any change in requirement interpretation forces re-evaluation of impacted tests. This is especially important for subtle architecture-dependent obligations such as server-side-only processing, synchronous malware scan before persistence, and dynamic UI refresh without page reload, because these controls can be partially implemented while still appearing correct from a shallow functional perspective.

Operationally, pull requests must reference the issue ID and impacted requirement keys. Automated test suites executed in CI must publish results against those same keys, allowing reviewers to confirm that changed code remains covered by relevant validation assets. Peer review will require explicit confirmation that new or modified acceptance criteria are either linked to existing tests or accompanied by new tests before merge approval. Defect triage will also reference trace keys to distinguish a requirement miss from an environmental failure or data issue. This model supports change impact analysis and reduces the risk of undocumented regression when gateway rules, file-validation libraries, CDN policies, or auth middleware evolve.

### 4.3 Test Management
Test management for this release will follow a controlled lifecycle that begins with requirement intake, continues through design, implementation, execution, defect handling, re-test, regression, and concludes with evidence-backed exit assessment. Jira will serve as the workflow backbone for user story state management, while Xray or an equivalent test case repository will maintain logical test assets and execution associations. Test planning will create suites aligned to Component/UI, API, Integration, E2E, Security, and Performance layers. Daily coordination will review changes in environment health, blocker status, defect aging, and the readiness of external dependencies such as malware scanning, CDN purge observability, and cloud storage audit logging. Each execution cycle will classify results by environment, build number, data set, and network profile to preserve reproducibility.

CI/CD integration is mandatory. Pull requests targeting the protected branch must pass linting, unit/configuration tests, key PyTest API validations, and critical Playwright smoke journeys before merge. Nightly QA pipelines will execute broader regression packs including binary-validation partitions, auth-state permutations, and UI synchronization checks. Pre-Prod pipelines will add performance, CDN, and security validations where runtime parity is sufficient. Any failed critical gate—such as 401 contract regression, incorrect 5MB threshold behavior, malware-positive persistence, or stale-avatar propagation—must block promotion until triaged and resolved or explicitly waived through approved governance.

Peer-review discipline is part of test management, not separate from it. Code changes affecting upload handlers, auth middleware, file validators, storage adapters, CDN purge logic, or avatar rendering components require at least one reviewer from engineering and one reviewer accountable for quality or release risk. Reviewers must confirm that associated tests were updated, that observability remains intact, and that no architectural violation was introduced. Human-in-the-Loop approval gates remain enforceable: the pipeline pauses for architecture validation before full orchestration and again before release authorization when risk posture and unresolved defects are reviewed.

#### 4.3.2 Test Repositories
Test materials will be stored in version-controlled repositories under the same governed source control ecosystem as the application, with branching standards aligned to release policy. UI automation assets, API/service test suites, reusable fixtures, binary header-validation datasets, mock contracts, and performance scripts will be organized by capability and execution layer. Ownership will be shared between the QE lead and repository maintainers, while merge permissions will remain protected through pull-request review policies. Artifact discoverability is essential: test assets must be named with requirement alignment in mind so that reviewers can easily identify the scripts covering authentication gating, 5MB boundary checks, crop persistence, malware verdict handling, and CDN synchronization.

Repository maintenance will be tracked through commit history, pull-request discussion, code owners, and branch protection. Changes to reusable fixtures or network-conditioning profiles will require extra scrutiny because they can affect multiple suites simultaneously. Historical script updates will remain visible to support defect root-cause analysis and auditability. Generated reports are not to be manually edited in-source; instead, pipelines will publish immutable artifacts linked back to repository revisions.

#### 4.3.3 Recording Pass/Fail Results
A test case will be marked Pass only when all expected assertions succeed and required evidence is captured without contradiction across linked observability layers. For example, a valid avatar upload passes only if the UI interaction succeeds, the gateway returns the expected response, malware scanning returns a clean synchronous verdict, storage persistence is confirmed in the correct bucket path, CDN invalidation is observed, and the updated avatar renders dynamically in target UI surfaces without a full reload. Partial success is insufficient. If one downstream dependency fails or produces ambiguous evidence, the result must not be recorded as Pass.

A test case will be marked Fail when expected behavior is not achieved, when contradictory evidence appears, or when the system violates a requirement despite completing the apparent user action. Thus, a file that uploads but persists before malware verdict, or a successful upload that leaves stale CDN content in header views, is a failure. Blocked status will be used only when environment, access, tooling, or external dependency issues prevent valid execution. Pass/fail criteria will be standardized in the test repository and linked to Jira execution records to ensure consistency across manual and automated runs.

### 4.4 Defect Tracking
Defect tracking will be managed in Jira using a disciplined workflow tailored for release risk visibility. Defects will be logged with severity, priority, environment, build number, reproducibility, traceability linkage, evidence artifacts, and suspected component ownership. Minimum ticket content will include reproduction steps, expected versus actual behavior, impact statement, screenshots or logs, and cross-links to the failed test case or pipeline execution. For this release, special tags will identify defects affecting authentication, gateway validation, crop correctness, malware scanning, storage persistence, CDN invalidation, or SLA timing so that risk concentration becomes immediately visible in dashboards and triage sessions.

The workflow will follow states such as Open, Triaged, In Progress, Fixed, Ready for Retest, Retested, Deferred, and Closed. Severity classification will treat security bypass, unauthorized access exposure, malware-positive persistence, wrong-file acceptance, or widespread stale-avatar propagation as high or critical depending on exploitability and blast radius. Pull requests created to resolve defects must reference the Jira defect ID and the impacted requirement trace keys. No defect may be closed without evidence from retest, and no high-severity defect may be deferred without explicit approval from product, engineering, and release management. Daily triage will review aging, reopen rates, and environmental noise so that teams distinguish genuine product failures from unstable infrastructure.

### 4.5 Test Data Management

#### Data Requirements
Test data for this story includes authenticated and unauthenticated browser-session states, expired-session cookies, clean high-resolution JPEG and WebP images, exact 5.00MB boundary files, 5.01MB oversize files, malformed and truncated binaries, renamed text or script files carrying false image extensions, malware-positive image fixtures for safe sandbox scanning, crop scenarios with varying coordinate selections, and expected CDN-visible outputs. Data strategy must support repeatable execution without exposing production user assets. Where representative real images are needed, they must be masked, synthetic, or enterprise-approved sample media. Malware-positive fixtures must be controlled and limited to safe validation patterns allowed by the scanning sandbox.

Data lifecycle controls are important because binary test assets can drift over time. Versioned datasets will be maintained so exact file hashes can be referenced by automated tests. Seeding approaches will populate required user accounts, profile records, authorized bucket paths, and purge-observable CDN targets before execution. Cleanup routines will remove temporary uploaded objects and reset profile state where necessary to avoid false positives driven by stale cache or leftover assets. Sensitive session artifacts must never be hard-coded into repositories; secrets and tokens will be injected through secure pipeline variables or ephemeral setup services.

#### Data Availability
Test Type | Data Source | Environment | Data Type | Data Volume | Comments
---|---|---|---|---|---
Functional / UI | Synthetic avatar image set and session fixtures | QA, Pre-Prod | JPEG/WebP, cookies, DOM baselines | Moderate | Includes valid crop scenarios and dynamic refresh validations.
Negative Validation | Binary rejection dataset | QA, Pre-Prod | Oversize, malformed, spoofed, unsupported files | Moderate | Includes explicit 5.01MB gateway-drop inputs and UI banner verification.
Security Validation | Malware sandbox fixtures and auth variations | QA, Pre-Prod | Safe malicious indicators, expired/missing tokens | Low | Controlled access only; evidence retained securely.
Performance | Timed upload payload pack | QA, Pre-Prod | Clean 4G-profile upload binaries and telemetry samples | Moderate | Sized to cover below-limit and exact-limit cases.
