### 4.6 Test Environments
Execution requires multiple environment layers with explicit observability. The QA web application environment must support manipulation of browser cookie/session states so valid, expired, and missing-auth conditions can be exercised deterministically. An integrated API Gateway environment must expose request and response logging sufficient to confirm 401 rejection, size-limit drops, and backend routing behavior. Backend integration environments must demonstrate server-side-only execution and provide evidence that no client-side compression or canvas processing is invoked. A malware scanning sandbox must support synchronous clean and malicious verdict simulation. Cloud storage test buckets must exist in the authorized ecosystem with folder-level audit logs, while CDN test environments must provide purge-event visibility and edge-read validation.

Browser automation environments must allow no-reload detection through navigation timing, route inspection, or DOM mutation observation. Network-conditioned execution hosts will use tcconfig or equivalent controls to simulate standard 4G restrictions consistently. Pre-Prod should mirror QA logically but with deployment-equivalent CDN and storage topology to validate propagation timing more realistically. Environment readiness will be formally reviewed before execution, and any gap in logging, purge visibility, or storage auditing will be treated as a quality risk because it weakens evidence reliability.

### 4.7 Test Automation Strategy
Automation strategy for AD-488 is intentionally tool-specific and architecture-aware. Playwright is the primary framework for browser-driven validation because it can reliably assert hidden or disabled UI states, authenticate via controlled session setup, perform file-upload interactions, observe crop workflow behavior, and verify dynamic DOM refresh without triggering a full browser reload. It also provides screenshots, traces, and network-event evidence that are valuable in release triage. PyTest will serve as the backbone for API, integration, and file-validation automation because it supports parametrized payload matrices, straightforward binary fixture handling, and robust assertion patterns for HTTP contracts, storage-side effects, and service integration outcomes. Together, these frameworks create separation of concerns while preserving shared traceability.

JMeter will be used for end-to-end SLA timing and stress-oriented path measurement under bounded release conditions, particularly to observe whether upload transport, synchronous malware scanning, storage commitment, and CDN invalidation collectively remain within the 2.0-second target. tcconfig will configure deterministic network throttling to standardize the 4G condition required by acceptance criteria. OWASP ZAP will supplement the automation stack with authenticated security baselines and targeted checks against the upload surface, helping identify weak headers, unexpected responses, or exposure patterns that functional suites do not surface. The integration strategy places these tools into CI/CD in layers: lightweight Playwright and PyTest smoke suites on pull requests, broader nightly regressions in QA, targeted ZAP and JMeter runs in scheduled or pre-release stages, and release-blocking reporting for critical failures.

Operationally, automation contributions must follow the same peer-review and pull-request rules as product code. New tests or fixture changes require code review, traceability updates, and evidence that flakiness controls have been considered. Pipelines will produce standardized artifacts for dashboards and audits. When automation exposes environmental instability rather than product defect, the issue must still be tracked so recurring false signals do not erode confidence. This strategy aims for sustainable automation that provides actionable release intelligence rather than volume without interpretability.

### 4.8 Test Reporting
Test reporting will consolidate execution status, requirement coverage, defect trends, blocker conditions, and release-readiness indicators into a single operational view consumed by QA, engineering, product, and release management. Daily reports during active execution will summarize planned versus completed runs, pass/fail rates by layer, critical defect movement, environment incidents, and SLA findings. Reports must differentiate between functional failures, security findings, data/setup issues, and infrastructure instability so leadership can make informed decisions quickly. For this story, reporting will explicitly surface the status of authentication gating, 5MB boundary enforcement, crop correctness, malware pre-persistence control, CDN invalidation, and 2.0-second SLA compliance, because these are the areas most likely to influence release go/no-go.

Dashboard views will be linked to Jira and pipeline outputs to minimize manual status drift. Pull-request checks will show immediate smoke outcomes, nightly jobs will populate broader regression snapshots, and pre-release reports will aggregate trend evidence from QA and Pre-Prod. Human-in-the-Loop approval packages will include a concise executive summary, unresolved high-risk items, and direct links to evidence artifacts.

#### 4.8.2 Maintaining Test Results
Historical execution logs, pipeline artifacts, screenshots, browser traces, API responses, performance outputs, and security scan summaries will be stored in governed artifact repositories integrated with the CI/CD platform. Retention should support at least one full release cycle plus audit needs, with critical release evidence retained longer according to enterprise policy. Results must remain linked to build identifiers, source revisions, environment tags, and requirement keys so future investigations can reproduce or compare prior behavior.

Where storage constraints require pruning, raw artifacts may be archived while summary metrics remain indexed in dashboards. Manual result manipulation is prohibited; corrections must be made through rerun or documented annotation. This ensures the release decision record remains trustworthy.

### 4.9 Tools
Tool | Purpose | Usage in This Release
---|---|---
Playwright | Browser automation for modern web applications | Validates auth-gated UI visibility, avatar upload controls, crop workflow, oversized file banner behavior, no-reload DOM refresh, and synchronized avatar rendering across active views.
PyTest | API, service, and integration automation framework | Executes gateway contract tests, file-validation matrix coverage, storage assertions, malware pre-persistence checks, and CDN-related service verifications.
JMeter | Performance and SLA measurement | Measures end-to-end upload-to-distribution timing under shaped network conditions and supports repeatable non-functional runs.
OWASP ZAP | Security scanning and baseline web vulnerability assessment | Assesses authenticated upload routes for unsafe responses, weak controls, and web-security regressions in non-production environments.
tcconfig | Linux traffic control configuration utility | Applies deterministic 4G-like bandwidth, latency, and packet profiles to execution hosts so SLA evidence is consistent.
Jira | Work tracking and defect management | Maintains story workflow, defect states, severity, retest status, and release visibility.
Xray | Test management and traceability | Links requirements to cases, executions, and evidence for audit-ready coverage.
GitHub Actions | CI/CD orchestration | Runs pull-request checks, nightly regression, scheduled security/performance jobs, and artifact publication.

## 5. Resource Estimate / Roles
Execution is expected to require a compact but cross-functional team. Core staffing should include one QA automation engineer focused on Playwright and PyTest implementation, one performance/security-capable QA engineer supporting JMeter, ZAP, and evidence analysis, one manual/functional QA analyst for exploratory validation and business-facing UAT support, one SDET or Dev partner for integration observability and testability enhancements, and part-time support from DevOps/cloud engineering for gateway logging, storage audit access, CDN purge visibility, and network shaping infrastructure. Product and engineering leads remain essential for acceptance clarification and defect triage.

Estimated duration is approximately 10 to 12 working days after environment readiness and HITL approval. Days 1-2 cover final traceability alignment, data setup, and environment certification. Days 3-5 cover automation completion and targeted manual exploratory testing, including crop-specific validation enhancements and explicit oversized-banner assertions. Days 6-7 cover integrated QA execution and defect triage. Days 8-9 cover performance/security cycles and reruns in stable environments. Days 10-12 cover Pre-Prod confirmation, release reporting, and sign-off support depending on defect volume and dependency responsiveness.

