## 7. Entry and Exit Criteria
Entry criteria include approved scope boundaries, validated architecture-aligned test approach, available QA and Pre-Prod environments, accessible gateway logs, malware sandbox availability, storage audit visibility, CDN purge observability, provisioned test accounts, versioned data fixtures, and approved Human-in-the-Loop gate authorization. Builds must be deployable and sufficiently stable for repeatable execution.

Exit criteria require full coverage of critical acceptance criteria, successful execution of all priority-one automation and manual cases, no open critical defects, acceptable disposition of high defects with stakeholder approval, evidence that rejected files do not persist, proof that valid uploads propagate correctly without page reload, and confirmation that SLA results meet the 2.0-second target or are formally waived. Release recommendation will also consider defect reopen trends and environment confidence.

## 8. Assumptions and Dependencies
This strategy assumes that the malware scanning service provides deterministic synchronous verdicts in lower environments, that CDN purge events are observable, that test buckets mirror production folder logic sufficiently for audit verification, and that session management can be manipulated safely for valid and expired auth states. It also assumes crop behavior is implemented in a way that exposes a testable persisted result, whether via asset URL variation, metadata, or resulting rendered dimensions.

Key dependencies include API Gateway configuration parity, access to cloud audit logs, stable browser automation execution hosts, secure management of test secrets, and collaboration from engineering for observability gaps. If any dependency is unavailable, risk levels will rise and execution depth may need re-planning.

## 9. Risk Mitigation Table
Risk ID | Risk Description | Probability | Impact | Severity | Mitigation / Preventive Actions | Contingency / Recovery Actions | Owner
---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---
R1 | Authentication token expiration or cookie desynchronization causes inconsistent gating between UI and gateway, leading to false access or non-reproducible 401 behavior. | Medium | High | High | 1) Create deterministic session fixtures for valid, expired, and missing-token states. 2) Validate UI gating and gateway response in the same test flow. 3) Log token age and auth middleware decisions in QA/Pre-Prod. 4) Add pull-request smoke tests covering auth permutations. | Reissue test sessions, rerun with captured auth logs, isolate middleware regression, and block release if UI/gateway behavior diverges. | QA Lead / Auth Engineering
R2 | Dynamic database or profile-read synchronization lag causes updated avatars to appear in one surface but remain stale in header bars or active views. | Medium | High | High | 1) Add multi-surface assertions in Playwright for profile page, header bar, and active session views. 2) Correlate CDN purge events with profile-read timestamps. 3) Use cache-busting diagnostics for investigation only, not as acceptance behavior. | Trigger targeted cache purge diagnostics, review read-model latency, rerun synchronization suite, and hold release if stale-avatar exposure persists. | QE Lead / Frontend Lead
R3 | Mock or sandbox malware service desynchronization differs from integrated behavior, masking scan-before-store defects until late stages. | Medium | High | High | 1) Maintain contract tests against scan service responses. 2) Verify storage non-persistence on malicious verdicts in integrated environments. 3) Schedule early end-to-end runs with real sandbox dependency. 4) Tag scan-related defects as release blockers. | Escalate to dependency owner, switch to validated sandbox endpoint, rerun persistence checks, and prevent promotion without scan-path confidence. | Security QA / Platform Team
R4 | Oversized uploads near 5MB experience gateway or client rounding inconsistencies, causing 5.00MB and 5.01MB boundary misclassification and incorrect banner behavior. | Medium | High | High | 1) Use hash-verified binary fixtures at precise byte counts. 2) Test below, exact, and above-threshold files across UI and API layers. 3) Assert explicit mapping from gateway rejection to UI banner for 5.01MB cases. 4) Include regression checks in PR and nightly suites. | Freeze release candidate, inspect gateway byte-calculation logic, correct threshold handling, and rerun all size-boundary suites before approval. | QE Lead / API Team
R5 | CDN invalidation latency or purge event loss causes SLA breaches and stale public avatar rendering despite successful storage. | Medium | High | High | 1) Capture timestamped checkpoints for upload, scan, store, purge, and visible render. 2) Validate purge acknowledgements and edge-read freshness. 3) Execute comparative runs in QA and Pre-Prod. 4) Use JMeter plus Playwright correlation reporting. | Re-run purge verification, engage CDN operations, defer release if invalidation remains nondeterministic, and document affected user impact. | Release Manager / DevOps
R6 | Crop coordinate mapping or server-side crop persistence defect results in the wrong image region being saved or the original asset being rendered after success. | Medium | Medium | Medium | 1) Define crop test oracles with known source images and expected output regions. 2) Validate persisted cropped variant via rendered result, dimensions, or metadata. 3) Cover edge coordinates, center crop, and near-boundary selections. 4) Include peer review for crop-specific assertions. | Quarantine crop-dependent release scope, reproduce with fixture set, compare original versus persisted asset, and require fix validation before go-live. | QA Analyst / Image Service Team
R7 | Performance under shaped 4G conditions breaches the 2.0-second SLA because combined gateway filtering, scanning, storage, and purge latency accumulates unexpectedly. | Medium | High | High | 1) Use tcconfig to standardize network shaping. 2) Instrument step-level timings in JMeter and service logs. 3) Identify latency contributors early in QA rather than only in Pre-Prod. 4) Establish release threshold reporting with trend comparison. | Escalate bottleneck owner, optimize offending step, rerun SLA suites, and block release or secure explicit waiver if target remains unmet. | Performance QA / Engineering Lead

